#
# Counter-example: a boundary math:CellIncidence and its declared transpose coboundary
# math:CellIncidence (linked by math:adjointIncidence) carry DISAGREEING ±1 signs (+1
# vs −1), so the coboundary matrix is not the transpose of the boundary matrix and the
# adjunction δ = ∂* fails. It fires math:BoundaryCoboundaryAdjunctionConstraint
# (shacl.SPARQLConstraintComponent → math:BrokenBoundaryCoboundaryAdjunction) and NO
# other finding: both incidences are complete (coface, face, sign), and the two are on
# disjoint cells so they form no codimension-2 boundary composition the general-sum
# constraint would score.

@prefix math: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/math/> .
@prefix ex:   <http://example.org/math/adjunction-inconsistent/> .

ex:cX a math:Cell ; math:cellDimension ex:dim2 .
ex:cY a math:Cell ; math:cellDimension ex:dim1 .
ex:cZ a math:Cell ; math:cellDimension ex:dim1 .
ex:cW a math:Cell ; math:cellDimension ex:dim0 .
ex:dim0 a math:NaturalNumber ; math:inNumberSystem math:naturalNumbers ; math:isExact true .
ex:dim1 a math:NaturalNumber ; math:inNumberSystem math:naturalNumbers ; math:isExact true .
ex:dim2 a math:NaturalNumber ; math:inNumberSystem math:naturalNumbers ; math:isExact true .

# The boundary entry [∂cX : cY] = +1 and its declared adjoint coboundary entry, whose
# transpose sign should also be +1 but is authored as −1 — the adjunction is broken.
# The two incidences share no chaining endpoint, so no boundary composition forms.
ex:iBoundary a math:CellIncidence ;
    math:incidenceCoface ex:cX ; math:incidenceFace ex:cY ; math:incidenceSign 1 ;
    math:adjointIncidence ex:iCoboundary .
ex:iCoboundary a math:CellIncidence ;
    math:incidenceCoface ex:cZ ; math:incidenceFace ex:cW ; math:incidenceSign -1 .
